{
 "id": "2022-zhang-greener",
 "name": "Greener Streets and Tourists' Emotions in Xi'an",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: participants who have never visited Xi'an see the paper's 18 street photos (Figure 1, CC BY 4.0) in random order. Nine are control photos of culture and leisure walking streets, traffic lanes and commercial pedestrian streets in central Xi'an. The other nine show the same views with greenery added at three scales (green view index up 5–9.9%, 10–14.9% and 15–20%). Each photo stays at least 10 s and is rated on the study's eight 5-point word pairs: pleasure (repulsive–nice, tense–restful, uncomfortable–comfortable, unpleasant–pleasant) and arousal (monotonous–exciting, inactive–active, boring–interesting, fearful–safe). Analysis as in the paper: pleasure and arousal means, paired t-tests of treatment vs control, a mixed model with a treatment dummy, image fixed effects and a participant random intercept, then split by greening scale and street type. Original study: 299 Chinese potential tourists; greenery raised pleasure by 1.21 and arousal by 1.24 points.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the paper's online questionnaire showed each photo for 10 s; here the photo stays on screen with the word pairs and Continue unlocks after 10 s; (2) the intro and the instruction line are mine, following the paper's \"imagine walking along the street\"; (3) the \"never visited Xi'an\" filter is part of the consent statement; (4) education categories are my wording, and employment and field of study are not asked.",
